scala_mcp_tool — wrap any classpath method as an mcp tool (tier 2)
====================================================================

status: planned

spec: "scala-backed mcp tools: scala_mcp_fun and scala_mcp_tool"
(D-2026-08-19-scala-backed-mcp-tools). decided 2026-08-19, NOT scheduled
into a wave. investigation notes: LINTER_FINDINGS.md.

TIER 2 of two. this plan removes tier 1's precondition. plans/scala_mcp_fun
can only wrap a function its author already registered as a Scala.Fun;
this one wraps ANY class + method on the classpath, so it reaches
components whose author never heard of us.

    scala_mcp_tool lint = \<open>isabelle.linter.Linter.lint_snapshot\<close>
        (description \<open>lint a theory\<close>)
        (params theory: string)

DEPENDS ON plans/scala_mcp_fun and does not restate its steps. that plan
settles the error mapping, the registry/activation story, and the fact
that ML can call scala from inside a tool body at all. what is new here
is one Scala.Fun of ours and the reflection behind it.

it does NOT inherit tier 1's calling convention. tier 1's D1 makes
declaration ORDER the contract and accepts that nothing checks it —
defensible there because its audience wrote both the Fun and the
declaration. tier 2's whole point is code whose author never heard of
us, so that trust is unavailable. what tier 2 inherits is the PROBLEM
statement: mcp arguments arrive named, scala wants them positional, and
something must bridge that. reflection is how tier 2 pays for it —
see D1 below and its open question Q4.

WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. the isabelle linter is the motivating case and it
is exactly the kind tier 1 cannot reach: it ships
`isabelle.linter.Linter_Tools` (a CLI service) and `isabelle.linter.Lints`
(its own lint extension point), but no Scala.Functions service. Its
engine is one pure function over the object our server already holds:

    (* isabelle-linter linter_base/src/linter.scala:131 *)
    def lint_snapshot(snapshot: Document.Snapshot,
                      lint_selection: Lint_Store.Selection): Report

we must not link against it and it must not know about us. reflection is
what makes both true at once.

NON-GOALS. no enumeration of what is on the classpath — the user names
the target. no attempt to serve the CLI plane (`isabelle lint` and
friends): tool bodies print to stdout and stdout is our transport, and
external tools call sys.exit in their own closure, so that plane stays
discovery-only (see LINTER_FINDINGS.md section 3). no linter-specific
code anywhere in mcp.jar — if this plan ends up mentioning the linter in
source rather than in tests, it has failed.

the one new scala function
--------------------------

    MCP.dynamic_call   (a Scala.Fun in mcp.jar, ours, the only one)

    1. split the target into class + method
    2. Class.forName(cls, true, Isabelle_System.classpath().class_loader)
    3. .getField("MODULE$").get(null)      -- scala object singleton
    4. reflect the signature; fill each parameter (D1); invoke
    5. render the result (D2)

it must extend Scala.Fun DIRECTLY, not Fun_String. this is the load-
bearing detail of the whole plan:

    (* Pure/System/scala.scala:22 *)
    abstract class Fun(val name: String, val thread: Boolean = false) {
      def invoke(session: Session, args: List[Bytes]): List[Bytes]
    }

Fun_String and Fun_Strings are conveniences that DROP the session. we
need it: ML cannot express a Document.Snapshot, but the session can
resolve one by theory name, and without that lint_snapshot is
unreachable no matter how good the reflection is.

