DEVIATIONS from the plan as written, each a deliberate choice:

- D1 gained an AMBIENT rule the plan did not have, and it is the one that
  makes the linter reachable without naming it. lint_snapshot's second
  parameter is Lint_Store.Selection, a type mcp.jar cannot mention. The
  rule: for an unknown parameter type, look for a companion
  `apply(Options)` and build it from the session's own options
  (Session.session_options). Generic -- it is the ordinary Isabelle idiom
  for a configuration object -- and it needs no coercion table entry per
  component. Without it the plan's D1 would have had to special-case the
  linter, which the NON-GOALS forbid.

- D2's coercion table landed only as String/Int/Boolean. Path,
  List[String] and List[Path] were NOT built: nothing on the path to the
  linter needs them, and the plan's own rule is that an unbuildable type
  is a loud error naming the type. Add them when a target wants them.

- D3 rendering is better than toString but still not structured. A result
  that is a case class renders by FIELD NAME (scala 3 keeps
  productElementNames), and fields holding collections are dropped --
  without that a lint Result drags its whole Parsed_Command and Snapshot
  into every row. Honest status: readable, not machine-parseable.

- Step 1 (the scala_mcp_tool outer syntax) IS NOW BUILT, after the note
  below was written. It declares, validates the target at registration
  (a bad target fails there with a position, not at call time), surfaces
  the resolved scala signature in the tool description -- the only place
  a client can see the target's real types -- and serves. Cases 7a-7d of
  mcp/test/repro_dynamic_call.py.

  TWO findings from building it:

  * The ML -> Scala bridge IS available at theory BUILD time, not only in
    a live session (verified by building a Pure-based probe theory whose
    body calls Scala.function1 and asserts the result). That is what makes
    registration-time validation legal: a theory declaring a
    scala_mcp_tool can still be built by isabelle build.

  * An Isar-declared param CANNOT be named `theory`. The params clause
    parses names with Parse.name, and `theory` is a command keyword, so it
    is rejected -- as is `text`. This is pre-existing behaviour of the
    params clause, not something this command introduced, but it bites
    here because `theory` is the natural name for the snapshot selector
    AND the name the scala-side builtins already use. Declarations say
    `thy`; dynamic_call accepts theory/thy/theory_name so the raw-ML and
    Isar paths both work. Worth knowing for plans/ml_builtin_migration,
    which will hit the same wall when it moves a builtin carrying a
    `theory` param into the ML registry.

  The note below stands as the reason step 1 was not blocking: The existing mcp_tool ML escape hatch
  already wraps dynamic_call into a served tool:

      mcp_tool lint = \<open>fn thy => hd (Scala.function "MCP.dynamic_call"
        ["isabelle.linter.Linter.lint_snapshot", "theory", thy])\<close>
        (description \<open>style lints for a loaded theory\<close>)

  declared via repl_step, `lint` appears in tools/list (38 rows) and
  tools/call returns findings. So the linter IS reachable through the MCP
  today. What step 1 still buys is ergonomics and declaration-time
  validation -- MCP.check_target is built and returns the parameter list,
  so its backend is waiting.

- A1 IS met: the three resolution failures produce three different
  messages ("no class X", "found X but it is a class, not a scala object",
  "class X has no method Y; it has ..."), each tested. Getting there also
  narrowed the catches from Throwable to ClassNotFoundException /
  NoSuchFieldException -- a blanket catch there would swallow
  Exn.Interrupt and turn a cancelled call into a bogus resolution error,
  which matters given the plan already records that a lint holds the
  prover thread for its whole run.

- T1's own-jar targets live in mcp_test, not mcp: mcp.jar ships no test
  code (.claude/rules/scala-rebuild.md). This costs nothing and is in
  fact a better test -- mcp_test.jar is reached by exactly the same
  reflection over the same classpath as a stranger's jar.

FOUND LIVE, and it blocked everything until fixed: mcp/etc/settings
carried `ISABELLE_CLASSPATH="...:$COMPONENT/lib/mcp.jar"` on top of
build.props's own `module = lib/mcp.jar`. A component's module is already
put on java.class.path, so the jar was there TWICE, Classpath.services
scanned it twice, and every service class was instantiated twice. That
was merely cosmetic while the component only declared tools (the
duplicate `mcp_server` row in `isabelle -?`), but became FATAL the moment
it declared Scala functions: ML builds those into a Symtab and the
repeated name raises `DUP "MCP.dynamic_call"` at session startup. The
line was removed separately, as 64a703d (PR #7) -- so this plan DEPENDS
on that fix rather than carrying it.

ALSO FOUND: `isabelle lint` is NOT a usable control in this environment.
Run against the same fixture through its own CLI it reports nothing,
while our snapshot path reports four findings -- its batch route
reconstructs a snapshot from Export.MARKUP, which this build does not
populate. Do not use CLI agreement as the oracle for a snapshot-path
test; read lints.scala and assert named lints instead, which is what the
fixture and repro now do.

