decisions and assumptions
-------------------------

(testing process: bridge cases in mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala
extending MCP_Session_Suite; the scala-unit layer over Fake_Backend can
carry the pure reflection cases, which need no prover. see plans/README.
IMPORTANT: the linter is NOT installed in this environment and must not
become a test dependency — target our OWN jar's classes for every
mechanical case, and keep the linter to one optional, skipped-if-absent
integration case.)

D1  the fill rule, and the reason a signature CHECK is worth having at
    all. UNLIKE tier 1, this does not rest on declaration order — the
    method's own signature is readable, so use it. each parameter is
    filled from one of two sources, decided by its type:
      EXPLICIT  String, Int, Boolean, and anything in the coercion
                table (D2) <- the ML call args, by parameter NAME
      AMBIENT   Document.Snapshot, Session, Options, Sessions.Deps
                <- server state, never from ML
    an ambient parameter that needs a selector (which snapshot?) takes
    it from an explicit param by convention: a Document.Snapshot is
    resolved from a `theory` argument. a parameter that is neither
    explicit nor ambient is a registration error naming the type.
    this is what turns "call string functions" into "call
    lint_snapshot", and it is why the check is dispatch and not merely
    validation.

D2  coercion is the real work, not the plumbing. ML hands over strings;
    a real target wants List[Path], Options, Severity.Level,
    Lint_Store.Selection. ship a SMALL explicit table (String, Int,
    Boolean, Path, List[String], List[Path]) and make everything else a
    hard registration error naming the type it could not build. do NOT
    add a reflective bean-style fallback: a wrong coercion that
    type-checks is worse than a refusal. generic parameter types survive
    erasure in the Signature attribute, so getGenericParameterTypes
    distinguishes List[Path] from List[String] — VERIFIED 2026-08-19 by
    javap on our own mcp.jar (`find_section(List<Doc_Catalog$Heading>,
    String)`), so the table can key on the real type and not on `List`.

D3  result rendering: call toString on the returned value, EXCEPT where
    the target type has a known renderer. rationale: a Report or a
    case class renders as scala noise, which is useless to an agent.
    where the plan cannot do better, say so in the tool description
    rather than pretending the output is structured. (this is the
    weakest part of the design and the honest place to admit it — a
    tool whose output is `Linter$Report@3f2a` has advertised a
    capability it does not have.)

D4  overloads are REJECTED, not resolved. getMethod needs parameter
    types; the declaration supplies names and arity. if more than one
    method matches, fail at registration listing the candidates and
    require the declaration to disambiguate. guessing here would bind a
    tool to a method the author never meant.

A1  [scala unit] a target naming a missing class, a missing method, or
    a class with no MODULE$ (i.e. a scala CLASS, not an object) each
    fail at REGISTRATION with a message naming which of the three went
    wrong. the three messages must differ — "reflection failed" is not
    actionable.

A2  [scala unit] the class/method split is ambiguous and the resolution
    is longest-class-first: `a.b.C.d` tries class `a.b.C` method `d`
    before class `a.b` method `C.d`. a target that resolves to neither
    lists both attempts. (this is a small thing that will otherwise
    produce baffling errors for nested objects.)

A3  [scala unit] MODULE$ is how a scala object is reached — VERIFIED
    2026-08-19 against mcp.jar: `object Doc_Catalog` compiles to class
    `isabelle.mcp.Doc_Catalog$` carrying
    `public static final isabelle.mcp.Doc_Catalog$ MODULE$`. the test
    pins this against our OWN jar so it fails loudly if a scala version
    bump changes the encoding.

A4  [bridge] the AMBIENT fill works: a target taking a
    Document.Snapshot is called with a `theory` argument only, and the
    snapshot it receives is the one the session holds for that theory —
    asserted by having the target return something only a real snapshot
    can produce (its command count), cross-checked against the same
    count read through an existing tool. this is D1's load-bearing
    claim and the one that makes the linter reachable.

A5  [bridge] the session a Scala.Fun receives IS the server's live
    session, not a fresh or stale one. asserted by resolving a theory
    that was loaded mid-session, after the Fun was registered.

T1  [bridge] the minimal round trip: scala_mcp_tool over a
    String-in/String-out method of our OWN jar, declared in a theory,
    advertised in tools/list, called, correct value back.

T2  [scala unit] the coercion table (D2): each supported type converts,
    and an unsupported type fails at registration naming the type.

T3  [scala unit] overload rejection (D4): a target with two matching
    methods fails at registration listing both.

T4  [bridge] error mapping end to end: a target that throws surfaces as
    an mcp isError with the CAUSE unwrapped, not a bare
    InvocationTargetException. the server survives and answers the next
    call.

Q1  PARTLY RESOLVED 2026-08-19 — the linter IS now installed here, so
    the integration case is available. corrections to what this plan
    first said: the release has ZERO assets (it is a source tag, not a
    prebuilt component), so it must be BUILT — plain `isabelle
    scala_build`, which works fine under the flatpak and takes seconds.
    register `linter_base`, not the repo root. the jar reaches the
    classpath from build.props `module` alone, with no `classpath` line
    in its settings — verified by `isabelle -?` serving `lint`,
    `lint_bundles`, `lint_descriptions`, and by `isabelle lint_bundles`
    returning the five bundles.
    STILL OPEN: the test case ships SKIPPED-IF-ABSENT regardless, so the
    suite stays green on a machine without the component.

Q2  OPEN: whether MCP.dynamic_call should cache resolved Method handles.
    reflection lookup per call is wasteful but the target set is tiny
    and correctness comes first; measure before caching, and note that
    a cache keyed on the target string must be invalidated if a
    post-boot jar load is ever added (LINTER_FINDINGS.md section 6.3 —
    post-boot loading is visible only to its own loader, so a cache
    would silently serve the wrong class).

Q4  RESOLVED 2026-08-19, POSITIVE — parameter NAMES do survive into the
    bytecode, so D1's fill-by-name works as written. `javap -v` finds a
    MethodParameters attribute carrying real scala source names on both
    jars tested:

      isabelle.jar  isabelle.Doc$             path0, env, ml_settings, path
      mcp.jar       isabelle.mcp.Doc_Catalog$ acc, session, variant_names

    so java.lang.reflect.Parameter.getName() returns `ml_settings`, not
    `arg0`.

    the part that GENERALISES, and the reason this is not a lucky local
    build: ISABELLE_SCALAC_OPTIONS carries no -parameters flag at all
    (`-encoding UTF-8 -feature -java-output-version 21 -source 3.3
    -old-syntax -no-indent -color never -pagewidth 78 ...`), yet the
    attribute is emitted anyway. mcp.jar is built by `isabelle
    scala_build` with exactly those options, which is the same path any
    component jar takes — so a third-party component's prebuilt jar, the
    linter's release included, carries names too.

    LIMIT of the evidence, stated so nobody over-reads it: the BEHAVIOUR
    is confirmed, the CAUSE is not. it was not isolated whether this is
    a scala 3 default or a consequence of -java-output-version 21, and
    only scala 3.3.4 as bundled was tested. a jar built entirely outside
    the isabelle toolchain by a build that suppresses the attribute
    would lack names.

    CONSEQUENCE for the design, and it de-risks this plan rather than
    merely unblocking it: names are an ENHANCEMENT, not a precondition.
    when present, fill by name and let a mismatch fail at registration
    with both name lists shown — the checkable contract tier 1 can never
    have. when absent, fall back to declaration order, which is exactly
    tier 1's rule. so tier 2 = tier 1's ordering plus a verifier that
    degrades cleanly, and no jar can block it.

Q3  OPEN, inherited from plans/scala_mcp_fun A3: the blocking cost is
    WORSE here than for tier 1, because a realistic target (linting a
    whole theory) runs for seconds rather than microseconds with the
    prover thread held. this may make async MCP.run_tool
    (plans/ml_builtin_migration) a PREREQUISITE rather than a
    companion. decide after A3's measurement in the tier-1 plan.

